# Migrations + persistence

This rule scopes AGENTS.md hard rules **5, 6** and the Don'ts about
migration-editing and ORM-pickling.

## Hard rules

1. **Migrations are immutable once committed.** Never edit a shipped
   file under [alembic/versions/](mdc:alembic/versions). Add a new
   one. Treat each shipped migration as a permanent historical
   record — even if it has bugs, ship a follow-up that compensates.
2. **Naming convention.** `00NN_<short_slug>.py` — increment `NN` to
   the next free number, slug is lowercase / hyphens optional /
   under ~25 chars (e.g.
   `0028_analysis_layer.py`).
3. **Autogenerate is a starting point, not a finish line.** After
   `alembic revision --autogenerate -m "msg"`:
   - Inspect indexes (autogenerate often misses them).
   - Inspect check constraints.
   - Inspect server defaults — they trip up SQLite test runs.
   - Confirm down-revision points at the previous head.
4. **Update docs alongside the migration.** Adding a table or column
   means a one-line addition to
   [docs/data-dictionary.md](mdc:docs/data-dictionary.md) and an
   ERD entry in [docs/erd.md](mdc:docs/erd.md) in the same PR.
5. **All ledger writes go through
   [`LedgerWriter`](mdc:aqp/persistence/ledger.py).** Never insert
   directly into ledger tables from a route, task, or subsystem
   helper. The writer enforces tenancy stamping, audit fields, and
   FK invariants.
6. **Don't pickle ORM objects across tasks.** Pass IDs; re-fetch in
   the worker. Celery serialises the message; detached ORM
   instances pickled this way break in subtle, lazy-loading-related
   ways under load.
7. **ORM model files are split by domain.** Add new classes to the
   right `aqp/persistence/models_*.py` (`models_bots.py`,
   `models_rl.py`, `models_analysis.py`, `models_sinks.py`,
   `models_streaming_links.py`, `models_producers.py`, …).
   `aqp/persistence/models.py` is for cross-domain primitives only.

## Adding an ORM model — the checklist

1. Class lives in the appropriate `aqp/persistence/models_*.py`.
2. Generate a migration:
   ```
   docker exec aqp-api alembic revision --autogenerate -m "add foo"
   ```
3. Inspect + fix indexes / constraints / defaults in the generated
   file.
4. Apply: `docker exec aqp-api alembic upgrade head`.
5. Update [docs/data-dictionary.md](mdc:docs/data-dictionary.md).
6. Update the per-domain ERD in [docs/erd.md](mdc:docs/erd.md).
7. If an agent will read it, add a `DataMCPTool` — see
   [data-mcp.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/data-mcp.mdc).

## Recovering from a busted migration

```
docker exec aqp-api alembic downgrade -1
# fix the migration file
docker exec aqp-api alembic upgrade head
```

This is the **only** time you edit a migration after writing it —
when it has not yet been committed and is still on your branch. Once
merged, fix forward.

## Don't

- Don't edit a shipped migration. Ever. Add a new one.
- Don't run `alembic stamp` to "skip" a migration in production
  databases without a runbook.
- Don't introduce `JSONB` columns where a typed column would do —
  agents will see them and try to query the wrong shape.
- Don't bypass `LedgerWriter` to "save a roundtrip". Telemetry +
  audit FKs depend on it.
- Don't query Postgres from agent code — go through DataMCPTool. See
  [data-mcp.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/data-mcp.mdc).
